1
00:00:00,345 --> 00:00:01,560
One demonstration.

2
00:00:02,243 --> 00:00:06,142
A robot plans the motion, transfers the interaction, and checks progress

3
00:00:06,162 --> 00:00:06,859
through touch.

4
00:00:08,349 --> 00:00:12,722
Here, a person presses and twists a safety cap, resets their grip, then

5
00:00:12,742 --> 00:00:13,509
removes it.

6
00:00:14,245 --> 00:00:17,003
The demonstration gives us the structure of a task.

7
00:00:20,345 --> 00:00:21,884
Each segment becomes a node:

8
00:00:21,929 --> 00:00:26,854
approach, grasp and press, twist, reset and regrasp, check release, lift, and

9
00:00:26,874 --> 00:00:27,755
place aside.

10
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:30,093
The agent plans free motion.

11
00:00:30,775 --> 00:00:33,754
At contact, it preserves object-relative geometry.

12
00:00:34,424 --> 00:00:36,064
Touch governs transitions.

13
00:00:36,734 --> 00:00:38,910
Placement belongs to the human demonstration;

14
00:00:39,089 --> 00:00:40,696
the robot run ends at lift.

15
00:00:42,349 --> 00:00:45,313
Perception locates the object and follows the hand.

16
00:00:46,049 --> 00:00:47,896
Masks separate image regions;

17
00:00:48,039 --> 00:00:50,323
keypoints describe hand configuration.

18
00:00:51,073 --> 00:00:54,548
These overlays connect the task stages to visible evidence in the

19
00:00:54,568 --> 00:00:55,517
demonstration.

20
00:00:58,349 --> 00:01:02,673
At contact, the hand's position and orientation are expressed relative to the

21
00:01:02,693 --> 00:01:03,400
object.

22
00:01:04,136 --> 00:01:07,598
A gripper adapter maps this relation to robot geometry.

23
00:01:08,347 --> 00:01:11,469
Grounding it in the current scene produces a robot target.

24
00:01:14,349 --> 00:01:17,167
For free-space stages, the agent sets a goal.

25
00:01:17,915 --> 00:01:21,777
A motion planner connects that goal to the robot's current configuration.

26
00:01:24,347 --> 00:01:28,220
The tactile library branches into images, forces, and torques.

27
00:01:28,923 --> 00:01:31,348
Each supports different contact primitives.

28
00:01:32,054 --> 00:01:36,686
For this bottle, select contact, pressing, contact retention, and release.

29
00:01:37,393 --> 00:01:40,658
The recorded controller uses changes in raw force channels.

30
00:01:41,364 --> 00:01:44,549
Torque is a possible input, but was not used in this run.

31
00:01:46,346 --> 00:01:48,877
The selected primitives become task conditions.

32
00:01:49,576 --> 00:01:52,004
This illustrative policy shows the composition:

33
00:01:52,225 --> 00:01:56,505
wait for pressing evidence, rotate while contact remains, reset and regrasp

34
00:01:56,525 --> 00:01:59,171
when needed, then verify release before lifting.

35
00:01:59,874 --> 00:02:02,680
The recorded hardware executor is task-specific.

36
00:02:04,346 --> 00:02:05,960
Now the robot executes.

37
00:02:06,647 --> 00:02:08,914
Pressing evidence permits the first twist.

38
00:02:09,606 --> 00:02:11,070
The first turn feeds down;

39
00:02:11,257 --> 00:02:13,089
later turns follow the thread upward.

40
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:18,077
Between segments, the robot unloads, resets its wrist, and regrasps.

41
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:22,888
Six ninety-degree segments produce five hundred and forty degrees, with five

42
00:02:22,908 --> 00:02:24,579
automatic reset cycles.

43
00:02:26,346 --> 00:02:28,541
Turn count alone does not permit lifting.

44
00:02:29,248 --> 00:02:33,036
Resistance must build, then stay low in fresh measurements while contact

45
00:02:33,056 --> 00:02:33,769
remains.

46
00:02:34,468 --> 00:02:36,686
A stationary check confirms release.

47
00:02:37,384 --> 00:02:40,987
The final reading falls below the recorded threshold, and the controller

48
00:02:41,007 --> 00:02:42,739
permits a thirty-millimetre lift.

49
00:02:44,349 --> 00:02:48,285
Tactile Agent connects demonstrated intent and object-relative interaction

50
00:02:48,305 --> 00:02:51,936
with robot motion, supervised by task-specific touch.
